{-
   What a **body** does wrong with a `@lazy` parameter - `doc/spec/functions.md`'s Deferred
   arguments. `Reject.Deferred.yana` holds what a declaration does wrong, and the two cannot share a
   file: resolution's error checkpoint is what this walk runs after.

   # resolver: ownership

   Two kinds, and only the first is about `@lazy`.

    - **At most once on any path**, which is what makes this call-by-name rather than call-by-need:
      forcing twice is rejected rather than supported, so there is no memoization slot, no flag and
      no allocation anywhere in the lowering. Asked of the finished body, so `if c: v else: v` is
      once and a read inside a loop is twice. Reading it is also the whole of what forcing is, which
      is why handing the thunk on to another deferred position spends the same one permission.
    - **A thunk is a closure**, so the two shapes a closure cannot have in this stage are the two a
      deferred argument cannot have either. Those name the milestone they wait for rather than the
      marker.

   Written in `I32` for `Baseline.yana`'s reason: this stage does not build Core.
-}

fn bump(mark: I32) -> I32 = mark

fn passed(flag: Bool, @lazy v: I32) -> I32 = if flag then v else 0

-- Twice in a row, which is the plain case.
fn readTwice(@lazy v: I32) -> I32 = v + v

-- Twice on one path through a loop. The rule is about paths and not about the text, so this is the
-- same defect as the one above rather than a second one.
fn readInLoop(count: I32, @lazy v: I32) -> I32:
    let &total = 0
    let &i = 0

    while i < count:
        total = total + v
        i = i + 1

    return total

-- Handing the thunk on **is** the one read, so a body that also uses the value has read it twice
-- even though it never ran it.
fn handOn(flag: Bool, @lazy v: I32) -> I32 = passed(flag, v) + v

-- Not storage. What lives there is the thunk, and the value only exists once it has run - so there
-- is nothing for an assignment to land in.
fn assigned(@lazy v: I32) -> I32:
    v = 3
    return 0

-- The two shapes a closure cannot have in this stage, reached through the thunk rather than through
-- a written lambda. A thunk inside a thunk is built since J139 where what it names is the enclosing
-- thunk's own - `passed(True, passed(True, bump(1)))` compiles - so the nested case here names a
-- parameter from outside both, which would be a capture of a capture.
fn nested(n: I32) -> I32 = passed(True, passed(True, n))

fn inGeneric(x: a) -> I32 = passed(True, bump(2))

{-
   The same rule over an **instance member's** body, which is where it has to be asked separately.

   The marker is the class's and the body is this implementation's, so a member that inherited the
   position inherited the promise with it - and nothing about being reached by selection makes
   forcing twice cost less. `Class.yana` is the accepting side.
-}
data Gate = Gate {depth: I32}

class Guard(a):
  fn pick(self: a, @lazy v: I32) -> I32

instance Guard(Gate):
    fn pick(self, v) -> I32 = v + v

fn main() -> I32 = 0
